※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=6 diag=none

※ What a macro has to be able to do before it can take a template apart.
※
※ **`⎕name` is what a piece is written as, as text**: a string literal answers what is
※ between its quotation marks and a name answers itself.  **`$(EXPR)` where the expression
※ answers text puts the literal a program would have written**, which is the rule already
※ there for a number with one more type in it.
※
※ And in between, a macro walks text and joins it -- which needs three things of the machine
※ that runs one.  A string literal is a variable in the image, so the machine has to put
※ what a variable starts out holding into its own memory and answer an address for it.  A
※ join calls the allocator, which is per-target assembly with no body to run, so the machine
※ is the allocator while a macro runs.  And the join itself is ordinary code in the module,
※ which the machine already ran.

@[impure]
macro twice(t: syntax) → syntax:
    let said: str = ⎕name(t)
    let out: mut str = ""
    foreach c: char = said:
        ※ Reading the character as well as counting them, since walking text is the
        ※ half of this a template needs.
        if c ≠ 'b':
            out ← out ⧺ said
    ⌜$(out)⌝

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u6:
    ※ "abc" once for every character of it that is not a 'b', which is twice -- and the
    ※ macro read the characters to know that.
    let s: str = twice⌜"abc"⌝
    ⎕narrow(⎕drop(#s), ⌜u6⌝) ?? 1u6
